{"description":"Prove, on real ARM hardware from the actual fleet rather than on a development laptop, that the audio path decided in walkie/adr:002-runtime-stack is buildable and fast enough.\n\nThis is the only item in the MVP whose failure changes what the MVP contains, which is why it runs first and blocks sto:voice-note.\n\nIt exists because walkie/fnd:terminal-audio-constraints establishes that no viable pure-Go Opus encoder exists, making a CGO dependency unavoidable — an unavoidable dependency must be tested, not assumed.","investigationNotes":"Planned investigation, to be replaced by observations as the spike runs.\n\n1. Build the audio module under the `voice` build tag for linux/arm64 using zig cc as the C compiler, and confirm the resulting binary runs on a fleet ARM device.\n2. Capture audio through the miniaudio binding on that device and confirm the input device is detected without any GUI or desktop audio stack present.\n3. Encode 20 ms frames with libopus at 16 kHz mono and measure the per-frame encode cost on that hardware.\n4. Confirm the default build — no `voice` tag — still cross-compiles as a pure-Go static binary with no C toolchain involved at all.\n5. Record whether the terminal in use reports key-release events under the Kitty keyboard protocol, to establish whether hold-to-talk is available as an enhancement anywhere in the fleet.\n6. Note total binary size and resident memory for the audio build on the smallest device in the fleet.","conclusion":"Pending — this spike has not been run. The conclusion is written when it completes, and walkie/fnd:terminal-audio-constraints is revised with the measured figures at that point, replacing its currently calculated latency budget."}